  {
    "episode_id": 84091,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84091.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "Well, I did. I hosted during the 1990s after I I ran for Congress a couple of times, as you know, and lost. But I had opportunities to do a radio show, and it ended up being syndicated all over the state of Indiana. And I was kind of like Rush Limbaugh on decaf. I mean, I'm someone you know me well, and you embody this as well, Jason. I'm conservative, but I'm not a bad mood about it. I mean, I don't begrudge people holding different views from me. And I tried to reflect that. I think I might have put that into practice on the radio program. I love having a good debate. I love having a good discussion. And frankly, as I wrote in my book, which I don't know if I mentioned, I wrote an autobiography. I learned lessons the hard way. My first few campaigns got pretty rough, pretty negative. And I got to the end of my second campaign in 1990, and I had time to really reflect. I'd made a decision to put my faith in Jesus Christ when I was in college. My Christian faith's the most important thing to me, but I looked at the campaign that I had run and it didn't really square with what I professed about how I'm supposed to do onto others. And I ended up writing an essay entitled Confessions of a Negative Campaigner in 1991. I was just getting it off my chest. But ended up getting reprinted in a lot of newspapers in Indiana. And I just thought if I'm back in the public debate, I always want to carry myself in a way that. that treats others the way I want to be treated, that hits hard on the issues, but hopefully shows a generosity that honors God. And so for me, I tried to make that define my radio show, and then with the opportunity to run for office, came around again. I always aspire to that, not perfectly, but I always aspire to. to do that. I think democracy depends on heavy doses of civility. And I expect we're going to get back to that, sir. I really do.",
