    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84091.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "Well, I'm very proud and I appreciate you giving credit where credit is due to Secretary DeVos and in our Department of Education. She'd been a champion of school choice all of her life. When I was running the transition in early 2017, late 2016 after the election, I called Secretary DeVos and asked her to come in because she's just got such a heart for education in the country. And so it was a natural extension that with regard to members of this association, we would just continue to champion that. I think it's been disappointing to see the current administration reverse course on that in many respects, and particularly the avalanche of regulations that have flowed out of this administration in a broad range of areas. The foundation I created has published recently kind of a seminal piece of work on What's largely been an uncovered story, you will live it and dealing with regulatory world. But it's that, you know, it's that kind of the story of, you know, Gulliver's travels. It's one little lash. after another that ties down opportunities. But I think help is on the way, and I speak not so much of this November, as maybe before the month is out, because I think this Supreme Court is going to overturn the Chevron decision and reset the balance between the Congress of the United States and the bureaucracy i mean it's it's remarkable to think about it for decades and i know you had presentations on that today uh... i was i was talking to your general counsel about it and uh... I told him I have no inside knowledge, obviously, but my instinct is that we have a majority on the court that sees the excesses of the administrative state. If you read Justice Neil Gorsuch before we pointed it with the Supreme Court, it actually written in the entire book. on the administrative state. He's largely considered the leading authority on that. And you know, you know, Jason, you and I differed on a lot of different issues, but we never differed on the principle that we were the lawmakers in Washington, that federal agencies don't make law. They apply the law and unpack it, and they're supposed to do that, and it's a proper role. But ever since the Chevron deference became part of the law of the land decades ago, institutions like yours, small businesses around the country, large businesses always were faced with, you begin with the assumption in the courts that whatever the regulatory agency said the law was is law. And I would tell you not to get too deep in the weeds, but I'm very optimistic that our Supreme Court is going to give us a fresh start. And I honestly believe that You know, the founders of this country that that hot summer in 1787, okay, it was in Pennsylvania. We can",
